<div class="mt-6 sm:mt-10" id="topic-tautologies">

    <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold">
        6. Tautologies
    </h2>

    <div class="w-full h-[1px] flex items-center justify-center pt-[10px]">
        <div class="bg-gray-600 w-full h-[1px]"></div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-0 sm:px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed break-words">A compound proposition is classified by its truth values across all possible cases. The three common classifications are tautology, contradiction, and contingency.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4"><table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm sm:text-base"><thead><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560] text-white"><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">Classification</th><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">Definition</th><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-2 sm:p-3 text-white">Tautology</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">True for every possible assignment of truth values.</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(p \lor ¬ p\)</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-2 sm:p-3 text-white">Contradiction</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">False for every possible assignment of truth values.</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(p \land ¬ p\)</td></tr><tr><td class="p-2 sm:p-3 text-white">Contingency</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">True for some assignments and false for others.</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(p \land q\)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-bold mt-6">Example: Law of Excluded Middle</h3><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-0 sm:px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed break-words">The expression \(p \lor ¬ p\) is always true because either \(p\) is true or its negation is true.</p><div class="overflow-x-auto mt-4"><table class="w-full text-center border-collapse text-sm sm:text-base"><thead><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560] text-white"><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(p\)</th><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(¬ p\)</th><th class="p-2 sm:p-3">\(p \lor ¬ p\)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-[#3A2560]"><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">T</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">F</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">T</td></tr><tr><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">F</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">T</td><td class="p-2 sm:p-3">T</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="text-md text-gray-400 px-0 sm:px-3 mt-3 leading-relaxed break-words">If the final truth-table column contains only T values, the expression is a tautology; if only F values, a contradiction; otherwise, a contingency.</p>
</div>
